      "extract": "Unlike Lie and Clifford algebras, there are only a finite number of Hurwitz algebras[^1]. These algebras are very generative. Given a Hurwitz algebra identified with a spinor space, one acts on this spinor space with the algebra itself, from the left, right, or from both sides. Sometimes this does not make a difference, and sometimes it does. Considering all the repeated nested actions generate the adjoint algebras,",
